Common mistakes include:
mixing units, such as millimetres and metres, in a calculation;
reading a drawing at the wrong scale;
memorising a detail without understanding why it is built that way;
ignoring tolerances, movement joints and drainage;
using an old code or another country’s regulation without checking;
estimating from a drawing that has no dimensions or notes;
leaving measurement and costing until the last week;
and treating software output as correct because it looks precise.

State your assumptions. A lecturer, like a site manager, needs to see how you reached an answer. If a number looks wrong, stop and check it before you carry it forward.
